      "content": "Yes, lowering the TTL before you start migrating is the standard best practice, but it works by making the \"wait\" shorter rather than forcing an instant global update.\n\nThink of it like giving the goblins a new, shorter expiration time on their sticky notes before you change the map. If your TTL is set to 300 seconds (5 minutes), the goblins are only allowed to keep the old information for 5 minutes. Once that time runs out, they *must* go back to the source and check the map again.\n\nSo, if you lower the TTL to a low number (like 60 seconds) before your migration starts, you are essentially telling the goblins: \"You can only hold onto this old address for a minute.\" When that minute is up, they will naturally refresh their data and see the new IP address you just published. This ensures that by the time you switch the records, the goblins are already ready to accept the new information.\n\nHowever, it doesn't \"force\" them to update instantly; it just ensures that when they *do* update, they do so quickly. If you change the TTL *after* the migration, the goblins will still be holding onto the",
